<h1>TActivePanel</h1>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.1" />
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.ActiveControls.TActivePanel" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TActivePanel</tt> is the active control counterpart of
<a href="?page=Controls.Panel">TPanel</a>. It renders the same <tt>&lt;div&gt;</tt> and
adds the ability to replace everything inside it during a callback.
</p>

<h2>Replacing the contents</h2>

<p class="block-content">
A panel does not expose a property that pushes a value. A handler calls <tt>render()</tt>
on the panel with the writer from the callback event parameter, and the panel renders
itself again and sends the result to replace the element in the browser.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
public function callbackRequested($sender, $param)
{
    $this-&gt;Panel1-&gt;render($param-&gt;NewWriter);
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
This is the way to change a region whose shape varies, such as adding or removing
controls, rather than changing one value inside it. Everything the panel contains is
rendered again, so a control the handler added to the panel appears in the result.
</p>

<h2>Child controls are not updated separately</h2>

<p class="block-content">
When a panel renders during a callback, it turns off
<tt>ActiveControl.EnableUpdate</tt> on every active control inside it. Those controls
would otherwise each send their own update, and the panel is about to replace the whole
region anyway. A handler that assigns to an active label inside a panel it also renders
therefore sees the value in the rendered output, not as a separate update.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
The replacement itself is subject to
<a href="?page=ActiveControls.Introduction#ClientSideUpdates">the five conditions for a
client-side update</a>. A panel with <tt>Visible</tt> set to false sends nothing, which is
why a hidden region is usually revealed by rendering an outer panel rather than the hidden
one.
</p>

